# Bound the tile work one composite may do, and make the fill-in look right.
# Merged from: webkit-tile-upload-budget-env, webkit-tile-upload-scroll-gate,
# webkit-tile-upload-nonblocking-settle.
#
# Device-root-caused (build 495): after each ~1 s main-thread pass commits
# screenfuls of CPU-painted tiles, the next composite (a) BLOCKS in
# waitUntilPaintingComplete for buffers Skia workers are still painting and
# (b) uploads tens of MB via glTexSubImage in one go (franceinfo layers up to
# 23460x1269). Compositor-thread samples showed the stalls inside driver-blob
# code / idle-waiting, FPS 0.2-3 during scroll. With WEBKIT_TILE_UPLOAD_BUDGET_MB,
# still-painting buffers and out-of-budget updates stay queued (never dropped)
# and renderLayerTree schedules follow-up composites to drain them. Lossy:
# stale/blank tile content for a few frames. Unset/0 = stock.
#
# Then two corrections that make it behave like other browsers:
#  - WEBKIT_TILE_UPLOAD_BUDGET_SCROLL_ONLY (default 1): meter only while the
#    scrolling tree reports motion (atomic stamp from scrollingTreeNodeDidScroll
#    + WEBKIT_TILE_UPLOAD_SCROLL_SETTLE_MS, default 250), and drain tiles ordered
#    along the scroll direction (leading edge first) so fill-in reads as a
#    progressive wave, not random squares popping in.
#  - The settled composite originally fell back to the STOCK drain — unbounded
#    glTexSubImage batch plus blocking on still-painting buffers — so every
#    settle flip stalled composition for the whole queued batch (the residual
#    franceinfo scroll freeze). Device A/B (fresh-load continuous scroll, build
#    509): non-blocking alone did NOT help (mean FPS 2.0 — byte volume, not the
#    blocking wait, dominates); metering every frame gave mean 7.4. So settled
#    composites are metered too, at a larger WEBKIT_TILE_UPLOAD_REST_BUDGET_MB
#    (ships 16, 0 = unlimited), and never block on still-painting buffers (they
#    defer to the TileDrain follow-up composite).
#
# Must apply AFTER webkit-composite-scroll-sync and webkit-fling-throttle-env
# (ThreadedCompositor.cpp / ScrollingTree.* contexts overlap).
#
# 2.54 rebase: tile updates are no longer processed inside the layer flush;
# CoordinatedSceneState drains every layer whose backing store reports
# hasPendingUpdates() on EVERY composite, so a TileDrain composite needs no
# special handling in CoordinatedPlatformLayer any more (the old hunks there
# were dropped). Upstream's new m_hasPendingTileUpdates flag is cleared after
# each drain, which would strand budget-deferred updates, so the store now
# recomputes it from the tiles.
#
# ---
